      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To study coronary-myocardial reserve in patients with a programmed pacemaker."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Stress-echocardiography was performed in 64 patients with coronary heart disease (CHD). The stress was induced by a gradual increase in the frequency of electrostimulation (ES) by 10 imp/min from initial 90 imp/min to threshold value which was defined as the frequency threshold of myocardial ischemia induction (FT)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Registration of defects in local left ventricular contractility and cardiodynamics in frequent heart ES identified patients with predominant coronary and myocardial failure. Low FT (100-110 imp/min) indicates poor coronary reserve while a considerable rise of the end diastolic pressure in the left ventricle indicates limited myocardial reserve. Positive results of the test in isolated ventricular ES were obtained in 90.9%, in atrial ES in 72.7% of patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "As a highly informative and reproducible method, stress echocardiography can be employed for optimization of antianginal therapy in CHD patients with a pacemaker."
        }
      ]},
